#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edf0996-1268-55c3-8ab5-3631d19ef9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid (x, y) \\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168476} {:answer/id #uuid "b35744fe-9d10-5946-823b-f264ade78e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid y \\ne x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168477} {:answer/id #uuid "ce88ca63-536b-5943-8b0d-f190f7bf0323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid y \\ne \\pm  x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168478} {:answer/id #uuid "fe889821-b57e-50eb-b9d8-d2ec6ba7552d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid x^{2} + y^{2} \\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168479} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec4c708-6543-55db-a2c7-a5758dfcde2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid x^{2} + y^{2} < 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce88ca63-536b-5943-8b0d-f190f7bf0323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) \\in  R^{2} \\mid y \\ne \\pm  x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168478} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eeef050-35f9-5c63-81d0-182fdc9ef190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e58a710f-da20-5986-98eb-b29ea7b4485f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122474" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed746dc3-9f51-59f0-aead-0b4738bf53b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341098} {:answer/id #uuid "382a6ac4-4b0c-548d-ade1-b2566d6843b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341099} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6aca0e-c557-5f15-aa31-343047fba81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341100} {:answer/id #uuid "e8036336-f315-5293-b75b-8fb505c40d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341101} {:answer/id #uuid "13395398-ea67-55a6-a2fe-9a5f8f74f59e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6aca0e-c557-5f15-aa31-343047fba81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341100} :answer-field/id #uuid "72dd5def-6976-59bc-873c-3d8ebef8e768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76ab55a5-b7ae-513b-bef2-54d7a5d78561" :question/math-academy-id "q-122660" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4})\\,i+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e8fbc6-2055-580e-971b-59c91acb8375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341105} {:answer/id #uuid "a08b5f42-d6f8-51a1-a1c9-7d63d7fcbafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341106} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e35abc-3303-56bd-88a9-5caf5d89a794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341107} {:answer/id #uuid "d948be7d-56a0-5268-9c7b-1b3dc5eb032a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341108} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdbaf85-3d8b-522f-a03c-d8c7458ce51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a08b5f42-d6f8-51a1-a1c9-7d63d7fcbafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341106} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ff913a-8f18-55e8-bafd-bb9c42d01c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "086063e9-05eb-5d55-a741-bf5ec8233d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122685" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $T(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- $U(t)=2t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$\n- $V(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6092ce9c-b2fe-5009-a68b-85f3d536466f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341112} {:answer/id #uuid "5f363fab-f09e-5c7e-ba5f-9a7e0de9d322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341113} {:answer/id #uuid "31345475-d75e-55ca-a077-85af4281c264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341114} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5def73-abcc-57c9-a2f1-cae201603545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341115} {:answer/id #uuid "f58579e1-38d4-5abf-a391-0e05562d5170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f58579e1-38d4-5abf-a391-0e05562d5170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341116} :answer-field/id #uuid "194c90ba-8bf2-50fd-bd39-9c9544d43226" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6a5608a-0fba-5a52-ac34-08b868b05a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122691" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=(x+2){e}^{yz}$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea3adc76-c737-5032-855e-572384003f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/de222a9251e08861dd06fdee593cc10557f0be1d2c7d891395512d699b35aa63.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341119} {:answer/id #uuid "c2202c2e-575e-5ab0-91e3-25864435fc30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/4aeebc183f9a6676f7487fce53ec46ad0f2cc91a87847d8a230471dc638ea448.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341120} {:answer/id #uuid "d804cc89-6cb0-567a-8f85-fb68e8da8434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341121} {:answer/id #uuid "85538cb8-1a09-5134-b35e-4ce3eb95bc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/ff0774689ae7215b624ea7eb3bed8f68a673fba4bcc4ceeb73f82b9081c32d4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341122} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb0f591-3ae4-5e91-84c5-3ad30e823fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/11cfd599bbfe85c9f3c7a23d5b95c6bd2b3ff199c6c0a4777429123afc74cb8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d804cc89-6cb0-567a-8f85-fb68e8da8434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341121} :answer-field/id #uuid "13614c47-5b79-5bee-9be8-d11672cb5627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c6bc97f-7c2a-5b13-a87c-12910c19f149" :question/math-academy-id "q-122710" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,i+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061075/assets/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)"}]}
